<title>BELGIUM: EU accepts France Telecom budget payment.</title>
<headline>EU accepts France Telecom budget payment.</headline>
<dateline>BRUSSELS 1996-10-31</dateline>
<text>
<p>The European Commission said on Thursday that French government plans to include a transfer from France Telecom to government funds to reduce the 1997 budget deficit were acceptable.</p>
<p>&quot;Yves Franchet decided this morning to accept the French government proposal,&quot; a European Commission spokesman told a news conference. Franchet is the head of Eurostat, the EU's statistics body.  </p>
<p>France regards the 37.5 billion franc France Telecom transfer as vital in cutting its deficit to three percent of gross domestic product to qualify for the planned European single currency.</p>
<p>The payment would be in exchange for the French government assuming the state-owned telephone operator's pension liabilities.</p>
<p>Critics have said the one-off transfer smacked off creative accounting and ran counter to guidelines in the EU's Maastricht Treaty for countries to take sustainable economic measures to qualify for the planned 1999 start of the single currency.</p>
</text>
